--- ANSWER ---
The affinity experiment is valid but cannot by itself approve the seven-loop design. Windows documents that process affinity applies to **all threads in the process**, and newly created child/local processes inherit it; therefore existing and later LabVIEW execution-system threads?�including P=4 workers and an in-process GPU keep-alive thread?�should be constrained. NI says parallel-For instances are scheduled onto LabVIEW execution threads rather than bound directly to cores. However, GPU work executing on the device, kernel-mode driver activity, or vendor helper processes lies outside `LabVIEW.exe`?�s affinity; inspect child/helper processes separately. Python and out-of-process COM are also unconstrained and can consume the supposedly removed or retained CPUs, so pin Python to a disjoint, topology-aware set or at least record whole-system CPU load. A?�B?�A detects monotonic drift but gives only one 10-LP sample; repeat blocks or bootstrap per-frame paired deltas. Also reset/warm each harness after every affinity transition because persistent GPU/DLL state may make `harness ??base` non-comparable. Most importantly, this isolated one-COM-run-per-frame benchmark does **not** reproduce contention among seven concurrent loops, so it can reject the plan but cannot establish that seven loops meet budget. [Microsoft affinity documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-setprocessaffinitymask), [Microsoft multiprocessor guidance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/multiple-processors), [NI parallel-For scheduling explanation](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Do-Parallel-For-Loops-run-on-dedicated-CPU-cores/td-p/1727132)

**Topology answer:** `Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor` confirms `NumberOfCores=6` and `NumberOfLogicalProcessors=12`, but those properties provide counts?�not sibling mapping. Use Microsoft Sysinternals `coreinfo -c`, which explicitly maps logical processors to physical cores. If it shows adjacent siblings, removing LPs 10??1 gives `0x3FF`. If it shows split-half siblings `(0,6)??5,11)`, removing LPs 5 and 11 gives `0x7DF`; derive the mask from the actual Coreinfo row rather than assuming either numbering scheme. [Win32_Processor properties](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/cimwin32prov/win32-processor), [Microsoft Coreinfo](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sysinternals/downloads/coreinfo)

Use a workload-derived gate, not an arbitrary percentage: fail G9 if the 5-core condition?�s paired parallel-kernel p90 regresses by more than **10%** *or* causes the modeled seven-loop p90 to exceed its cycle deadline with at least 20% headroom. That failure means test lower `P` and six-loop operation under an actual concurrent-loop workload; it does not automatically prove whether reducing loop count or P is the correct remedy.
